# 5. I2C and SPI Communication Pins

## A. I2C (Inter-Integrated Circuit)

### Definition

I2C is a two-wire serial communication protocol used to connect multiple devices using the same communication bus.

Two wires are required:

* SDA (Data)
* SCL (Clock)

## Arduino UNO I2C Pins

| Pin | Function |
| --- | -------- |
| A4  | SDA      |
| A5  | SCL      |

## Example Devices

* OLED Display
* MPU6050 (Accelerometer + Gyroscope)
* BME280 Sensor
* DS3231 RTC Module

## Example Code

```cpp
#include <Wire.h>

void setup() {
  Wire.begin();
}
```

## Real IoT Use Case

**Environmental Monitoring Station**

Multiple sensors (temperature, humidity, pressure) communicate with the Arduino over the same I2C bus, reducing wiring complexity.

---

## B. SPI (Serial Peripheral Interface)

### Definition

SPI is a high-speed serial communication protocol that uses separate lines for data transmission and reception.

It requires four main signals:

* MOSI (Master Out Slave In)
* MISO (Master In Slave Out)
* SCK (Clock)
* SS (Slave Select)

## Arduino UNO SPI Pins

| Pin | Function |
| --- | -------- |
| D10 | SS       |
| D11 | MOSI     |
| D12 | MISO     |
| D13 | SCK      |

## Example Devices

* SD Card Module
* Ethernet Shield
* RFID Reader (MFRC522)
* TFT Display

## Example Code

```cpp
#include <SPI.h>

void setup() {
  SPI.begin();
}
```

## Real IoT Use Case

**IoT Data Logger**

An Arduino collects sensor data and stores it on an SD card connected through SPI while simultaneously transmitting selected data to a cloud platform.

